    feat(keychain-opaque-handle): Keychain ↔ KeyVault bridge with byte zeroization
    
    Why
    - G2.4 requires the macOS Keychain bridge to be reconciled with the
      opaque-handle rule: raw KEK / DEK / device-private bytes must never
      cross the JS/Rust IPC boundary. The existing `secret_get` returns
      `Vec<u8>` directly, so the project needs a single authorised crossing
      that loads Keychain bytes into the `KeyVault` and returns only an
      opaque `KeyHandleId`.
    
    What
    - Add `apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/crypto/keychain_handle.rs` exposing
      `load_kek_into_vault(key, vault)` (`secret_get` + `KeyVault::insert_kek`
      + byte zeroization) and `insert_kek_from_bytes(bytes, vault)` for
      callers that already hold byte material.
    - Rust-internal `KeychainHandleError`: Keychain passthrough,
      InvalidKeyLength (32-byte enforcement), KeyVault failure. Does NOT
      cross IPC — callers map to JS-visible `E11xx` / `E13xx` variants.
    - 3 cargo unit tests cover wrong-length rejection, successful insert
      with byte-buffer zeroization, and handle resolution back to the
      inserted KEK.
    - Update `docs/contracts/tauri-commands-v0.md` §6.0.1 documenting the
      single authorised crossing and the rule that `secret_get` MUST NOT
      surface KEK / DEK / device-private bytes to JS.
    - Create `packages/keychain-opaque-handle/docs/dev_log.md`.
